    "q-90451": {
      "dom_id": "step-q90451",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Given that $X$ is a continuous random variable with the following probability density function, compute $\\text{E}[2{X}^{2}+1].$\n\n$$\nf(x)=1-\\frac{x}{4},\\,1<x<3\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we simplify using the properties of expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[2{X}^{2}+1]=2\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]+1\n$$\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the second raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{2}\\cdot (1-\\frac{x}{4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}({x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{16}){|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]=\\frac{11}{3}$ into the simplified expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[2{X}^{2}+1] & =2\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]+1 \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{11}{3}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
